WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:04.890
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.290
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.850
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:09.130
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.270
And now we're doing the ultimate Office Lovers podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.770
Each week, we will dive deeper into the world of The Office with exclusive interviews,

7
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.970
behind-the-scenes details, and lots of BFF stories.

8
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.990
We're the Office Lady 6.0.

9
00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.680
Hello, everyone.

10
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:27.880
Hi there.

11
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:32.560
Today on Office Lady 6.0, Michael Scott's favorite concierge joins us.

12
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.360
That's right.

13
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.300
It's Wendy McClendon-Covey, and she's in the studio with us.

14
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.120
Oh, I loved this interview.

15
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.780
Wendy is so hilarious.

16
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.220
Well, back when we were breaking down Business Trip in Season 5,

17
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.760
Wendy sent us some audio clips about her time on the show as concierge Marie.

18
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.580
They were just fantastic.

19
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.600
But today, we got to have her in studio with us,

20
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.440
and she is exactly as fun and funny as you imagine.

21
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.900
And, you guys, our breakdown of Business Trip is such a fun episode.

22
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.460
So if you haven't heard it, it's on our website, officeladies.com.

23
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.180
Hot tip, at any point, if you ever want to go back and listen to an episode,

24
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.680
they're all on our website.

25
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.580
And Wendy is just fantastic in Business Trip.

26
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.620
But she's just crazy talented in every regard.

27
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:19.140
Yeah.

28
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.980
You might recognize her from her role as Beverly.

29
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.940
Over 10 seasons on the Goldbergs,

30
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.440
she was nominated for two Critics' Choice Awards,

31
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.540
or maybe you know her as Deputy Clementine Johnson from Reno 911,

32
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.660
the animated show Big City Greens,

33
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.600
and she worked with our fellow Office alums,

34
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.220
Paul Feig and Ellie Kemper on Bridesmaids.

35
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:41.740
That's right.

36
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:42.800
She was Cousin Rita.

37
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:44.080
So funny.

38
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.000
And now you can catch her on another hit television show

39
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.100
with another member of our Office family,

40
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.020
writer-producer Justin Spitzer.

41
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.360
She plays Hospital Administrator Joyce in St. Dennis Medical on NBC.

42
00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.260
And it's so good, you guys.

43
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:58.960
It's so good.

44
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.140
The cast is phenomenal.

45
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.540
The writing is hilarious.

46
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.600
We're watching it and loving it.

47
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:04.160
Yes.

48
00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.840
I love Alison Tolman.

49
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.540
She plays sort of the head nurse.

50
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.420
It's a beautiful show.

51
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.480
It really is.

52
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.500
It's so funny.

53
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.480
But then it also really packs a punch.

54
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.380
If you're not watching it, I highly, highly recommend it.

55
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:16.680
Yes.

56
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.440
You know, I met Alison one time, and she's so nice.

57
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:21.880
We're such fans of hers.

58
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.920
Lady, I hope we get a chance to talk to her someday.

59
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.260
I am angling to be on St. Dennis Medical.

60
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:27.960
Same.

61
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.120
In fact, you guys, Jen and I are both pitching ourselves for different roles.

62
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.680
Before we go to break, I thought we should have a little refresher

63
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.440
about Concierge Marie's scenes.

64
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.240
So you guys remember, Michael is really down because Holly was transferred.

65
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:48.700
So David Wallace sends him and Oscar and Andy to Winnipeg to close a sale.

66
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:54.020
And clearly, this is Michael's first time ever meeting a Concierge.

67
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.360
And he is, in his own words, blown away.

68
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:58.560
Let's hear it.

69
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.320
Where's the Concierge?

70
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.000
Yes.

71
00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.740
Wallace said there would be one of those.

72
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:03.280
Mm-hmm.

73
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:03.940
Bingo.

74
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.380
Follow moi, bros.

75
00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:06.940
Wow.

76
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.560
What about a nice sushi place?

77
00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.160
Maybe a place with a view?

78
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.100
Oh, Matsuki.

79
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.420
That's a good one.

80
00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.060
You may walk there if you wish.

81
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.260
Or you may take the number 17 bus until 9 o'clock.

82
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.100
Other than that, you can take the taxi and the number is right there.

83
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:27.680
Wow.

84
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:28.160
Wow.

85
00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.100
I'm blown away by this.

86
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.580
I, um...

87
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.140
This is great.

88
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:34.620
Thank you.

89
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:36.620
One final question.

90
00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.960
Where might you find yourself on a Winnipeg night like tonight?

91
00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.620
Oh, the Huntsman is good.

92
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:44.320
The Huntsman?

93
00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.720
Down here, the financial district.

94
00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:47.300
Mm-hmm.

95
00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.740
Michael is so easily impressed.

96
00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:52.800
It's so cute.

97
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.440
I know.

98
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.420
He's enamored by all of it.

99
00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.620
Like, the travel, the hotel, the concierge.

100
00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:04.400
And the way Wendy is playing the concierge, it's just like, it's so rote.

101
00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.980
Like, how many times has she recommended these restaurants?

102
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.520
And got out the map.

103
00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.300
And dropped about the bus.

104
00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:11.880
Mm-hmm.

105
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:12.500
Yes.

106
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.100
Well, as the night goes on, they have drinks.

107
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:18.400
And Michael continues to be very impressed.

108
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.200
How about this one?

109
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.100
It's Christmas Eve.

110
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:21.740
Mm-hmm.

111
00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.560
And everything is closed.

112
00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.400
And you need to get some dry cleaning done.

113
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.140
12 o'clock, midnight.

114
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:27.840
Where do you go?

115
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:28.420
What do you do?

116
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.220
What do you do?

117
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:30.660
Come on.

118
00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:31.580
What do you do?

119
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:37.020
Oh, Astro Cleaners on St. James Place is the only place that is open on that day.

120
00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:37.320
Unbelievable.

121
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.200
Unbelievable.

122
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.880
Michael is, he is so turned on by her knowledge.

123
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:44.980
Unbelievable.

124
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.620
She knows where the dry cleaners are at any point of any day.

125
00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:50.920
She knows where you can go.

126
00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.420
I love this episode.

127
00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.160
I love the storyline so much.

128
00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.900
And I loved our conversation with Wendy.

129
00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.900
She's a really special person.

130
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.600
And she shared a lot.

131
00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.700
And I can't wait for everyone to hear it.

132
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:03.800
Same, lady.

133
00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.240
Well, let's take a break.

134
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.100
And when we come back, Wendy is in the studio.

135
00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:19.750
Wendy.

136
00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:21.370
You guys.

137
00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:22.490
What's up?

138
00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.430
Oh, my head's going to explode with happiness.

139
00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:26.610
Just pure joy.

140
00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:26.850
Because I'm here.

141
00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:27.310
Yeah.

142
00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.550
I've been so looking forward to this.

143
00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.370
I have been looking forward to it as well.

144
00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.370
And now it's happening.

145
00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:34.190
And I can't believe I'm looking at you.

146
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:34.550
I know.

147
00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:35.310
Lady.

148
00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:35.930
Lady.

149
00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.250
It's a lot of emotion.

150
00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:42.770
When you got here, we were reflecting on the last time we saw one another in person.

151
00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:45.670
Because you were on The Office, but we were not in your scenes.

152
00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:46.370
Right.

153
00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.350
And so you remembered the last time we saw each other.

154
00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:51.130
Will you share?

155
00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:53.190
It was called The Fur Ball.

156
00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:57.950
And it was like a kitty rescue fundraiser.

157
00:05:58.070 --> 00:05:58.270
Yeah.

158
00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.990
And you were one of the hosts, I believe.

159
00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:01.730
Yes.

160
00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:06.150
I worked with Kitten Rescue, who puts on that fundraiser for years.

161
00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:12.390
And that's one of the many reasons why I love you guys is because you care about the little creatures.

162
00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:12.950
We do.

163
00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:18.570
Yes, and so do you, because if you follow you on Instagram, Wendy, you know your love of critters.

164
00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:19.950
I'm always feeding something.

165
00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:21.670
And that giant hawk.

166
00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:22.990
What was that thing?

167
00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.330
They are all around our neighborhood.

168
00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:25.810
What?

169
00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:30.430
And the carnage that happens in our flower beds is not pretty.

170
00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:30.830
I know.

171
00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:39.990
We were doing an edit session recently, and out my window, we were—remember Cassie, Angela?

172
00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:45.970
We were on Zoom, and all of a sudden, it was like a bird exploded in front of my window.

173
00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.970
And I think it's because a big, like, hawk came down.

174
00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:49.930
Dime-bombed it.

175
00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:51.230
That's happened in my backyard.

176
00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:52.830
Just like confetti of feathers.

177
00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:53.950
Oh, God.

178
00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:56.150
And there's nothing you can do.

179
00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:00.270
That was just Mother Nature sorting some stuff out.

180
00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.430
But it's a little brutal to watch.

181
00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:02.810
Yeah.

182
00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.710
I didn't know we were going to start this way.

183
00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:06.850
Hi, everybody.

184
00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:14.650
I was about to tell a story about when I watched a squirrel eat a hummingbird, and then I didn't realize squirrels eat hummingbirds.

185
00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.050
And then I decided not to tell it, and now I'm saying it out loud.

186
00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:24.730
Well, listen, nature is disgusting, and I prefer it when nature stays outside.

187
00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:25.730
Keep it outside.

188
00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.010
Do your thing outside.

189
00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.350
Keep it outside, nature.

190
00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.290
But squirrel is a great, great event.

191
00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:32.750
Yes.

192
00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:33.770
Yes, it was really fun.

193
00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.250
I still have a teapot I bid on at that auction.

194
00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:37.870
Really?

195
00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:42.930
And it's a cat, and the head comes off, and then you put it on, and the water comes out its mouth.

196
00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:43.770
So he's like, meow.

197
00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.010
Oh, my goodness.

198
00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:46.950
That's so funny.

199
00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.130
It's so adorable.

200
00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:49.930
I hope you use it all the time.

201
00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.370
Only for my friends when they come over.

202
00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.470
So, Wendy, when you come over, get ready.

203
00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.710
I'll remember that you said that.

204
00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:05.310
Wendy, we know that you sent in some audio clips when we broke down your episode, Business Trip.

205
00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.750
But we're just so excited to have you in studio.

206
00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.350
Will you tell us again the story of how you got your job on the office?

207
00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:13.930
Yes.

208
00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:14.510
Yes.

209
00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:17.230
So, it was kind of crazy.

210
00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:24.530
I had just been fired off a movie that I had auditioned.

211
00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.270
I had done all these things.

212
00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:27.530
They flew me out to Michigan.

213
00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.030
I was there one night.

214
00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:35.690
Got in the passenger van to go to set, and the money guy decided I was not pretty enough to do the role.

215
00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:40.950
So, I was put on a plane crying to go right back to L.A.

216
00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:41.590
So, wait.

217
00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:43.330
You are in the van.

218
00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:47.750
You get to set, and they say, I'm sorry, that's it?

219
00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:49.610
Don't go to your dressing room.

220
00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:50.970
Holy crap.

221
00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:52.590
That is so brutal.

222
00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:57.390
It was pretty brutal, and yet a blessing, because that movie went nowhere.

223
00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:57.950
Okay.

224
00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:04.430
And I would have had to be in Michigan for seven weeks waiting to film for only five days.

225
00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:06.070
Oh, come on.

226
00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:07.470
Five intermittent days.

227
00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:08.090
Oh, no.

228
00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:08.350
Yeah.

229
00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:08.950
Come on.

230
00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:10.270
So, that's fine.

231
00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.550
That's how the universe works.

232
00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:11.830
Right.

233
00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:15.810
You know, sometimes you're in the wrong place, and something horrible has to happen to get you in the right place.

234
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:26.070
So, when I land in L.A., I look at my phone, and my phone's blowing up, because I had gotten an offer to do The Office the next day.

235
00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.710
So, thank God I got fired, right?

236
00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:31.890
Because nobody saw that movie, but everybody saw this.

237
00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:32.470
Mm-hmm.

238
00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:32.830
Okay.

239
00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:33.730
That's right.

240
00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:34.370
Can you say the movie?

241
00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:35.930
You don't have—you know what?

242
00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:41.290
We're going to do a special podcast called Office Ladies Burn It to the Ground when we're in our late 60s.

243
00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:41.650
Yes.

244
00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:42.430
And you can come back.

245
00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:43.090
You can come back.

246
00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:44.950
Yes, and then I'll name names.

247
00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:49.110
Because we have some stories to tell, but we're putting them in our pocket for a few years.

248
00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:49.810
That's smart.

249
00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:50.230
Yeah.

250
00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:51.130
That's very smart.

251
00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.670
When we're really old and give zero Fs, we're going to burn it down.

252
00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:54.930
I like that.

253
00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:55.350
Okay.

254
00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:55.930
I like that.

255
00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:57.790
I'll bring the tequila for that episode.

256
00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:05.850
So, anyway, my whole thing was with Steve Carell, and we were in Winnipeg.

257
00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:09.330
So, I didn't get to meet you guys, which was stinky.

258
00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:13.630
So, we're filming at a hotel somewhere in L.A.

259
00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.170
Yeah, in downtown L.A.

260
00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:18.750
Kate Flannery drove by and said hello, so that was cute.

261
00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:19.390
Oh, that's so nice.

262
00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.610
And then—okay, so it was on a Friday.

263
00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:24.070
The weekend goes on.

264
00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:25.930
Monday, I come back.

265
00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:31.650
And during lunch, I'm in my trailer eating.

266
00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:35.110
I get a call that I'm not going to be on Reno anymore.

267
00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:36.210
Wait, what?

268
00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:39.050
So, I am sobbing in my trailer.

269
00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:39.750
Yes.

270
00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:41.290
Was on the set of The Office.

271
00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:41.750
Yeah.

272
00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:42.330
Yeah.

273
00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:44.730
So, I was like, ugh.

274
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:50.990
I mean, I was like, well, now I'm going to get fired from this job because I can't collect myself.

275
00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:52.470
It was terrible.

276
00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:56.490
But the experience was so fun.

277
00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:58.310
And Steve Carell is a doll.

278
00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:00.490
I love the way the episode turned out.

279
00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:04.990
I loved, like, I'm in my full uniform, but no shoes on.

280
00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:06.370
Kicking him out.

281
00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.070
Like, what did we do in there?

282
00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:09.430
Because you've dressed already.

283
00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:12.750
Yeah, I got dressed in a hurry to throw his shoes out the door.

284
00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:16.490
Was I just on a break and had to go back down to my desk?

285
00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:17.130
I don't know.

286
00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.950
We want to know where that hotel room came from.

287
00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:21.290
I guess you set that up.

288
00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:21.930
Yeah.

289
00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:24.110
Concierge Marie would have access.

290
00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:25.190
Would have access.

291
00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:26.310
You didn't go to his room.

292
00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:30.150
No, it was my, like, little lair or something.

293
00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:37.330
You know how, like, though, at hotels, they have that room where if you have a layover and you're like, hey, can I just go shower somewhere?

294
00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:38.510
And there's that room.

295
00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:39.050
There is.

296
00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:39.710
Yes.

297
00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:41.510
I don't know about the layover room.

298
00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:42.450
I've never heard this.

299
00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:42.770
Yeah.

300
00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:44.450
I did this once.

301
00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:49.390
I had a layover and I got a room, like, just for a few hours so I could shower.

302
00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:50.810
Yes, you can do this.

303
00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:52.150
What hotel chain was it?

304
00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:53.630
Well, it was right off the airport.

305
00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:55.390
Oh, sure, sure, sure.

306
00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.090
But I used to work at a hotel as well.

307
00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.890
And everybody knows, like, okay, let me look up.

308
00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:04.650
Oh, there's a room on the corner that nobody's in.

309
00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:06.510
If you want to go have a hookup.

310
00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.750
And the employees would do this.

311
00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:09.210
Oh.

312
00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:10.010
Disgusting.

313
00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:11.650
But, yes, they would do that.

314
00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:13.870
That is some good hospitality gossip.

315
00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:14.050
Yeah.

316
00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:16.990
Very unsavory.

317
00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.290
What did you do in the hotel business?

318
00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:24.170
I was a secretary back when they called them secretaries.

319
00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:28.830
And then I became, like, a salesperson for corporate sales.

320
00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:33.530
I tried to get people to come and stay in our shitty little hotel.

321
00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:33.970
I'm sorry.

322
00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:34.470
Can I swear?

323
00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:35.030
You can.

324
00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:35.310
Yes.

325
00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:35.570
Okay.

326
00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:38.590
So this was—I worked at a place near Disneyland.

327
00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:42.370
It was, like, the third worst Ramada in Anaheim.

328
00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:49.170
And it was just far enough—it was literally across train tracks from the rest of—

329
00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.890
Where everyone goes to stay.

330
00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:51.190
Yeah.

331
00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:55.510
Where tourist Anaheim turns into industrial Anaheim.

332
00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:55.850
Okay.

333
00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:58.130
So this was basically a trucker hotel.

334
00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:01.930
But we were trying to make it into a destination property.

335
00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:04.650
So we put in—it was terrible.

336
00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:06.450
It sounds like another series you could start in.

337
00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:08.010
How do you put a bow on that?

338
00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.250
That's a tough sell.

339
00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:17.390
We put a waterfall in the front to really offset the fact that we didn't have hallways,

340
00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:23.750
just, like, outside corridors, you know, next to the rock-breaking factory or, like,

341
00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:24.690
the gravel factory.

342
00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:25.370
Gang, gang, gang, gang.

343
00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.350
Yeah, it was horrible.

344
00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:27.870
But, boy, did we try.

345
00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:35.650
I read, though, that when you were doing that, you would then drive 35 miles to L.A.

346
00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:37.250
to take classes at the Groundlings.

347
00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:37.550
Yep.

348
00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:38.150
Yep.

349
00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:38.930
Wow.

350
00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:39.610
Yeah.

351
00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:43.470
And I'm still too stubborn to leave that area.

352
00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:44.790
So I'm down in Long Beach.

353
00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:45.670
I've never left.

354
00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:46.510
Oh, my gosh.

355
00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:47.890
Oh, my goodness.

356
00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.890
You drove all the way here from Long Beach?

357
00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:52.630
But that's because I'm too stubborn to leave.

358
00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:53.770
My parents are nearby.

359
00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:55.110
My mother-in-law is nearby.

360
00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:56.250
Everybody's nearby.

361
00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.170
I'm not leaving.

362
00:13:57.510 --> 00:13:57.650
Yeah.

363
00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.330
But, yes, could I make it more difficult?

364
00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.550
I guess I could if I, like, moved to San Diego.

365
00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.750
But, yeah, it's a haul.

366
00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:08.550
Listen, if my family lived somewhere here, I'd be where they are.

367
00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:08.770
Yeah.

368
00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:09.250
Yeah.

369
00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:12.070
But they're just all the way in Texas, and so I can't.

370
00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:12.610
You can't.

371
00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:13.850
But I get that.

372
00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:14.190
Yeah.

373
00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:18.370
Oh, well, thank you so much for driving so far with us today.

374
00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:18.650
Of course.

375
00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:19.550
I have no idea.

376
00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:20.810
No, I'm honored.

377
00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:21.330
Are you kidding?

378
00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:22.470
But, wow.

379
00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:25.470
So you were doing that drive to go take classes at the Groundlings.

380
00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:29.250
And sometimes we would get so nervous that we would just drive right past and go home.

381
00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:29.710
Stop it.

382
00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:29.950
Yeah.

383
00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:33.850
You'd make it all the way to Melrose and just say, I can't.

384
00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:34.630
I can't get out.

385
00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:34.890
Turn it around.

386
00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:35.190
Yeah.

387
00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:36.330
I'm too nervous.

388
00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:37.890
Yeah.

389
00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.070
I took classes there.

390
00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.450
What year were you there, Wendy?

391
00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:45.910
I started in 97, and you know how long it used to take.

392
00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:49.190
It would take like two years because the waiting list was so long.

393
00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:50.070
You'd be on the waiting list for so long.

394
00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.870
I think we bounced around around the same time.

395
00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:56.450
What is your kind of like actor-y backstory, Wendy?

396
00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.810
Where are you from, and how did you get here?

397
00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.230
Are you from Long Beach originally?

398
00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:00.650
Yeah.

399
00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:02.330
That's the only place I've ever lived.

400
00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.250
I never went away to school.

401
00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:05.830
I'm very boring.

402
00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:07.450
You're born and raised Long Beach.

403
00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:08.430
Long Beach.

404
00:15:09.110 --> 00:15:10.070
Long Beach.

405
00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:10.450
Yeah.

406
00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:13.570
When did you get the acting bug or the comedy bug?

407
00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:21.290
Very early in life, but I didn't start really pursuing it in earnest until I got married

408
00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:22.450
at 26.

409
00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:26.330
So that's when, you know, because my husband was like, yeah, do it.

410
00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:26.830
Do it.

411
00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:28.090
I believe you can do this.

412
00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:30.110
No one else in my life thought I could.

413
00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:35.690
So he was working a couple of jobs while I was taking classes at the Groundlings, and

414
00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:40.050
he would sit there and let me cut wigs on him and, you know, run lines with me and

415
00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:40.590
all this stuff.

416
00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.890
So I did the Groundlings a lot.

417
00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:47.290
I took classes at South Coast Rep, but I didn't go through the whole rep program.

418
00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:52.850
And then I just figured, well, I'll stay in this Groundlings thing until they vote

419
00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.630
me out, because that's how it works there, you know.

420
00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:56.870
I didn't make it that far.

421
00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.130
How far did you go?

422
00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:00.970
Lab, right, is before you go to.

423
00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:06.310
And then I had to, I got put on a list of repeat lab, and I just never went back.

424
00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:07.730
I went to ImprovOlympic.

425
00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:12.970
I started doing long-form improv, the Herald at ImprovOlympic, while I was on the wait list

426
00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:16.390
thinking I would always go back, like, oh, I'll do this while I wait.

427
00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:20.630
And then I just kind of met my people, and I loved it.

428
00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:23.170
And then I just stayed there and rode that out.

429
00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:26.070
And I was still doing shows there, even when The Office started.

430
00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:30.630
And that's where I met Kate Flannery, because we were in the show together called Bitch

431
00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:31.110
Planet.

432
00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:32.710
Oh, what?

433
00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:33.290
Yeah.

434
00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:34.330
Oh, my God.

435
00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:35.630
I bet that was edgy.

436
00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:37.410
Sundays at 10 p.m.

437
00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:38.350
No parking.

438
00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.280
Don't you miss those days, though?

439
00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:44.820
Like, it was so fun.

440
00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:45.820
It was so fun.

441
00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:51.520
Doing these silly little shows and begging people to come and, you know, getting so many

442
00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:52.300
parking tickets.

443
00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:53.960
But yeah, so I—

444
00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.540
So you were going to just stay there and ride it out.

445
00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.000
Yeah, and, like, when my time's up, my time's up.

446
00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.800
But then I got in the company, which was nice.

447
00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:06.740
But then I started working, and I couldn't do as many shows as I wanted to.

448
00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.580
So, but that's how that happened.

449
00:17:09.580 --> 00:17:13.880
And then I was very frustrated because I thought, well, maybe I started this too late.

450
00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.380
I'm not really getting anywhere.

451
00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:16.940
I don't have an agent.

452
00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:21.440
Everybody would be in the dressing room talking about all the auditions they were going on.

453
00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:25.360
And I was like, well, I didn't even get a phone call because I, you know, I don't have

454
00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:26.940
anyone repping me.

455
00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:34.440
And anyway, so I subbed for someone in a show, and that's how I got discovered to go audition

456
00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:35.280
for Reno.

457
00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:36.500
Right.

458
00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:38.280
And that worked out.

459
00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.560
How old were you when you got Reno?

460
00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.140
So I was probably 32.

461
00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:44.000
Yeah.

462
00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:48.400
Does that feel kind of like a big milestone break?

463
00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:50.740
Like, yeah, that was a big deal.

464
00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:51.940
That was a big deal.

465
00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:53.140
It's so funny, Wendy.

466
00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:53.600
Thank you.

467
00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:54.720
I'm such a fan.

468
00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:55.040
Thank you.

469
00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:58.640
But it was like, you know, basic cable.

470
00:17:59.220 --> 00:17:59.420
Yeah.

471
00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:00.060
No one thought.

472
00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:05.780
I mean, it was like just going and doing arts and crafts with your friends, you know.

473
00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:08.940
But I feel like people keep finding it, don't you?

474
00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:09.400
Like.

475
00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:10.860
It's never been off the air.

476
00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:11.260
Yeah.

477
00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:14.320
Like your show will never be off the air.

478
00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:14.680
Yeah.

479
00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:20.180
So people keep coming to it in waves, and it's always a new discovery.

480
00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:23.580
And is it coming back?

481
00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:25.320
Is the office coming back?

482
00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:26.620
In a new form.

483
00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:26.980
Okay.

484
00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:27.560
Yeah.

485
00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:34.900
It's like the documentary film crew has found a new subject, and it's a small newspaper company.

486
00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:38.200
That's kind of just dying, this paper industry.

487
00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:38.960
Newspaper industry.

488
00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:44.740
So it's like the idea is that it's like the same people who made the documentary of Dunder Mifflin

489
00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:49.980
are making this documentary of the small paper company and following the employees the way they did before.

490
00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:54.400
But there's not currently any like, it's not our characters.

491
00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:56.320
Like Pam Beasley doesn't show up or anything.

492
00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:56.680
Okay.

493
00:18:56.680 --> 00:19:02.680
So the through line is the documentary crew, not the people from Dunder Mifflin.

494
00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:02.860
Yeah.

495
00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:03.840
Oh, that's interesting.

496
00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:04.360
Yeah.

497
00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:04.520
Okay.

498
00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:11.600
I feel like Reno 911 also has the benefit of being very like, you can make really fun clips out of it.

499
00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.480
It pops up on my Instagram all the time.

500
00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:22.140
I mean, how many times have I seen your bit where you're doing the, you know what I'm going to say, probably, the dancing.

501
00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:23.220
DUI.

502
00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:23.900
DUI.

503
00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:25.160
The DUI thing.

504
00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:25.480
Yeah.

505
00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:28.900
And that was one of the first things I ever did for that show.

506
00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:29.360
Really?

507
00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:30.320
Which is crazy.

508
00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.760
Oh, that lives everywhere.

509
00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:33.640
That lives everywhere.

510
00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:34.380
That lives everywhere.

511
00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:38.840
And then we got to, you guys remember when Quibi came out, right?

512
00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:39.320
Oh, sure.

513
00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:40.080
Oh, right.

514
00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:40.540
Yes.

515
00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:43.520
So we got to resurrect it a little bit.

516
00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:51.920
And because of that, we, you know, we did a bunch of other episodes that folded while we were on set.

517
00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:53.120
Oh, my gosh.

518
00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:54.180
But the checks were.

519
00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:55.980
It went out of business.

520
00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:58.220
It went out of business whilst we were filming.

521
00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:01.420
But we just kept going because we figured, well, someone's going to pick it up.

522
00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:05.280
So then we knocked out a bunch of episodes and two more movies.

523
00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:06.960
Yeah.

524
00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.960
So it's like the gift that keeps giving and whatever.

525
00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:15.000
I mean, I'm thrilled that I get to, you know, hang out and play with my friends.

526
00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:19.700
But it's just weird how things are cyclical like that.

527
00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:20.400
And I don't know.

528
00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:20.960
Yeah.

529
00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:21.540
Very fun.

530
00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:23.580
Well, I have a question for you.

531
00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:25.340
It comes from Christine M.

532
00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:26.740
in Ann Arbor, Michigan.

533
00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:27.220
Okay.

534
00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:33.700
She says, for this episode, did you have to handle a lot of Steve Carell's ad-libbing?

535
00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:38.480
And if so, do you recall which was the scene that was the hardest for you to get through?

536
00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:39.980
Like, did you guys break?

537
00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.700
Like, I don't remember breaking.

538
00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:52.140
I do remember him ad-libbing a little bit while he was just asking questions because he was so thrilled to be with a concierge.

539
00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:52.500
Yes.

540
00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:53.260
You know?

541
00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:57.960
And you had her vast knowledge of, like, where you can get your dry cleaning done.

542
00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:01.520
Of Winnipegian, you know, hot spots.

543
00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:09.040
Well, you know, and after I visited Winnipeg, that whole thing became so much more funny to me because there's just nothing there.

544
00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:16.880
But I remember thinking, don't break because you are lucky to be here.

545
00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:18.760
So do not break.

546
00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:21.240
And Steve never breaks.

547
00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:22.160
He never breaks.

548
00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:22.780
He never breaks.

549
00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:24.380
And Oscar never breaks.

550
00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:26.520
And you had to be in scenes with both of them.

551
00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.360
Ed Helms will break.

552
00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:29.060
Ed does.

553
00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:29.320
Yeah.

554
00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:31.220
But those two are like statues.

555
00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.080
And Oscar is a machine.

556
00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:34.560
Yeah.

557
00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:36.820
Because he had a recurring thing on Reno.

558
00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:37.860
Yes.

559
00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:43.680
And he would say these things, and I would be, like, having a nosebleed trying not to laugh.

560
00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:47.940
Like, I'm going to have an aneurysm if I can't let this out.

561
00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:51.120
And when someone raises the bar like that, you just can't.

562
00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:52.540
And I'm a giggler.

563
00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:54.300
But, damn it, I'm not going to do it.

564
00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.180
I'm not going to ruin a take.

565
00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:01.260
Well, we've shared, like, we had to stop making eye contact with Steve.

566
00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:05.720
So, like, I don't know if you can really track where our eyes were, but I would look at his neck.

567
00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:08.360
Because, like, if I looked in his eyes, I was a goner.

568
00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:09.100
Yeah.

569
00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:13.720
That surprises me because you two were so, like, in the moment.

570
00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:14.140
I don't know.

571
00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:18.100
I'm very surprised to hear that you guys were our breakers.

572
00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:20.700
There's a lot of footage.

573
00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:21.960
Really?

574
00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:27.540
That they have for the blooper reel with me and Angela because we would crack up a lot.

575
00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:29.240
Well, we couldn't be next to each other.

576
00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:34.800
It was that sort of cumulative thing because if I even felt her shoulder, you know, I'd be like, oh.

577
00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:35.280
Yeah.

578
00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:40.900
You know, like, so then they just started putting us apart.

579
00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:51.480
I feel like concierge Marie and Pam have a similar thing, which is that we had to play it totally straight against Steve's zaniness.

580
00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:51.840
Yeah.

581
00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:55.340
You know, like, we had to just, like, stare at him.

582
00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:55.580
Yeah.

583
00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:58.080
And you stare at him so well.

584
00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:01.740
But what, I, why do you think she hooked up with him?

585
00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.600
We actually got a question about it.

586
00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:05.240
Here, somebody said.

587
00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:07.140
Oh, yeah, a few people wrote in.

588
00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:08.080
Tara F.

589
00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:12.900
from Tustin, California said, I need to understand more about concierge Marie.

590
00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:15.180
Why was she in the bar alone?

591
00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:17.380
Why is she attracted to Michael?

592
00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:22.120
Was she having a bad day at the concierge desk and Michael made her feel seen?

593
00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:24.060
Well, there is this.

594
00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:25.460
Yes, to all of that.

595
00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:26.600
You nailed it.

596
00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:37.720
There is a moment when you go back and rewatch it when he says, you know, after meeting concierge Marie that if you ever get to meet a concierge, it is intoxicating.

597
00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.960
And the camera rack focus and pushes in on you and you look bored out of your fucking mind.

598
00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:45.120
You were just like, oh.

599
00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:45.620
It's so great.

600
00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:47.460
It's so perfect.

601
00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:52.800
Well, I think the listener who asked that question really nailed it.

602
00:23:52.800 --> 00:24:00.380
And again, in hotels, I used to see employees just drinking alone after work.

603
00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:10.640
Like, you know, when you don't have anything to do and you can't make the drive home before you get a drink, you got to get a drink at 501.

604
00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:11.300
Yeah.

605
00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:12.000
You know what I mean?

606
00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:14.220
I mean, and the bar is right there.

607
00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:19.120
It's right there and you get a discount and something might happen.

608
00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:21.680
You know, I think it was boredom.

609
00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:25.700
I think it was like, well, there are worse things I could be doing.

610
00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:26.100
Yeah.

611
00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:30.780
But then, you know, listen, she threw his shoes out into the hall, I think.

612
00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:33.140
It didn't go well once they got behind that door.

613
00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:34.400
Empty calories.

614
00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:35.660
Oh, yeah.

615
00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:36.200
Yeah.

616
00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:41.280
Did you do a little bit of a Canadian accent for concierge Marie?

617
00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:43.400
We got mail and I think I heard it.

618
00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:48.300
Well, there is that kind of, I was trying to do how I hear it.

619
00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:48.700
Yeah.

620
00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:51.740
And that French-Canadian accent is a little nasal.

621
00:24:52.160 --> 00:24:52.500
Yeah.

622
00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:55.960
And, okay, dry cleaners.

623
00:24:56.200 --> 00:24:58.500
Like, it's clipped in a weird way.

624
00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:01.520
Now, maybe I just sounded like someone with a speech impediment.

625
00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:04.480
But that's how I hear that accent.

626
00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:06.460
So, I don't know.

627
00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:06.920
Yeah.

628
00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:08.040
I'm not saying it was good.

629
00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:09.900
I'm saying that I did that.

630
00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:10.280
Yeah.

631
00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:11.500
No, I liked it.

632
00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:12.060
I liked it.

633
00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:18.000
I, one time, did a press junket in Montreal and the gal that showed us around had sort

634
00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:19.340
of a similar sound.

635
00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:19.780
Yeah.

636
00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.560
And it was kind of a way she went up on the end of her words.

637
00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:23.920
Yeah.

638
00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:24.460
You know?

639
00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:24.860
Yes.

640
00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:25.200
Yeah.

641
00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.700
I thought it was great.

642
00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:31.860
I'll put that on my, the special skills section.

643
00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:32.140
Special skills.

644
00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:36.740
Put it right next to roller skating and that was my special skills.

645
00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:37.620
They're so bad.

646
00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:38.020
Always.

647
00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:39.720
Do you have special skills?

648
00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:43.620
Like, back in the day when you had that resume, what were your special skills?

649
00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:45.700
I am a pretty good roller skater.

650
00:25:45.820 --> 00:25:46.220
Okay.

651
00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:47.260
That's not a joke.

652
00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:47.940
I am too.

653
00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:49.360
I love roller skating.

654
00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:50.640
I love roller skating.

655
00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:52.520
What kind of skates do you have?

656
00:25:52.840 --> 00:26:00.100
Girl, I got my roller skates, holy crap, in the 90s in Huntington Beach at a roller skating

657
00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:02.680
shop that I'm sure doesn't even exist anymore.

658
00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:06.060
And they're like the old brown suede.

659
00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:06.880
Stop.

660
00:26:06.880 --> 00:26:09.800
And I'm on my, I don't know, third set of wheels.

661
00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:11.680
I'm Southern sometimes.

662
00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:12.000
Okay.

663
00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:17.460
I'm on my third set of wheels and they're the wide ones because I like to be outside on

664
00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:18.180
my skates.

665
00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:20.440
Indoor skates are more slick.

666
00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:20.900
Uh-huh.

667
00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:22.360
And that's fine.

668
00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:23.720
I'll go to a roller rink.

669
00:26:23.860 --> 00:26:24.400
That's fine.

670
00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:27.140
But I like the wheels that are made for outside.

671
00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:29.240
And then I have the big front stopper.

672
00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:30.460
Oh, that's smart.

673
00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:32.500
And then I have the little one on the back.

674
00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:33.680
I don't use it as much.

675
00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:35.780
You have one on the back?

676
00:26:35.900 --> 00:26:36.180
Yeah.

677
00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:38.680
That you can drag if you're going.

678
00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:39.100
Oh, wow.

679
00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:39.780
I know.

680
00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:40.960
That's hardcore.

681
00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:43.100
But I don't use it very often.

682
00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:46.880
I have some pink suede moxies.

683
00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:48.320
Oh, moxies.

684
00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:49.040
Those are nice.

685
00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:49.380
Yeah.

686
00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:51.380
I mean, I have a few other pairs, too.

687
00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:54.720
I have some sparkly ones that Jessica Simpson sent me.

688
00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:56.360
Did you know she made roller skates?

689
00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:57.000
I did not.

690
00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:58.520
My mind is blown.

691
00:26:58.880 --> 00:26:58.980
Uh-huh.

692
00:26:59.300 --> 00:26:59.480
Yeah.

693
00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:02.000
Ladies, can I say something right now?

694
00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:02.540
Yes.

695
00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:10.860
Is there like a movie, like part improv where you play sisters who roller skate?

696
00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:11.580
I mean.

697
00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:13.500
Is this meant to be?

698
00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:14.000
I think.

699
00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:14.680
Oh, my gosh.

700
00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.820
Jenna, you are on to something.

701
00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:17.160
Right?

702
00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:18.220
I'm seeing it.

703
00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:19.420
I'm having a vision.

704
00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:20.260
I don't know.

705
00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:24.320
I mean, I will come skate through the halls of St. Dennis Medical with you.

706
00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:24.860
I mean.

707
00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:26.000
And we want this.

708
00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:26.460
You too.

709
00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:27.720
Look at you.

710
00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:28.740
Look at us.

711
00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:30.540
We should be skating together.

712
00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:31.440
We should be.

713
00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:31.820
Okay.

714
00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:32.180
Sorry.

715
00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:33.100
I got very excited.

716
00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.400
I got very excited about roller skating.

717
00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:49.560
You know, speaking of St. Dennis Medical, we got a lot of letters from people who are

718
00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:52.240
fans of the show and of you specifically.

719
00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:54.380
We have started watching it.

720
00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:55.660
We love it.

721
00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:56.420
It's so good.

722
00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:57.120
Thank you.

723
00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:58.480
What a phenomenal caSt.

724
00:27:58.800 --> 00:27:59.700
Thank you.

725
00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:00.300
Yes.

726
00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:01.960
You just got picked up for season two.

727
00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:02.640
Congratulations.

728
00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:03.300
Thank you.

729
00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:03.860
Thank you.

730
00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:04.380
Thank you.

731
00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:06.040
What can you tell us about working on it?

732
00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:09.240
Well, you guys know Justin.

733
00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:10.120
Mm-hmm.

734
00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:11.620
Justin Spitzer.

735
00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:14.560
And he is the loveliest man.

736
00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:15.540
So wonderful.

737
00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:16.900
Such a nice person.

738
00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:23.180
And for your listeners that have watched St. Dennis, Justin was in our episode where we

739
00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:27.920
had a guy, a performer, come and do something for our pediatrics ward.

740
00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:33.940
And he's the one that did a somersault and juggled and then walked off stage.

741
00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:35.080
That was our Justin.

742
00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:42.080
Um, I need to ask you about, in the very first episode, where you do a cartwheel.

743
00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:42.880
Right.

744
00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:45.860
And everyone makes an audible like, yes.

745
00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:47.540
Was that scripted?

746
00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:48.000
No.

747
00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.280
You just can do cartwheels?

748
00:28:50.380 --> 00:28:51.140
I'm so impressed.

749
00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.960
I can't do a cartwheel at my age.

750
00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:53.580
But you can.

751
00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:54.160
Can I?

752
00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:59.020
You can if you just decide to do it and you do it.

753
00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:07.100
Because what had been told to me by our showrunner, Eric, he came in and whispered in my ear,

754
00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.540
just be a real cheerleader about it.

755
00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:13.200
So I said, okay, I used to be a cheerleader.

756
00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:14.620
So that's what I'll do.

757
00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:17.920
So that's how I interpreted it.

758
00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:20.920
And he said later, well, that's not what I meant, but that was funny.

759
00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:24.120
Um, I mean, you really went for it.

760
00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:24.880
Yeah, I went for it.

761
00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:25.760
You do sell it.

762
00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:32.820
Well, listen, when you are trying to motivate a staff because you did something really dumb,

763
00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:36.080
like buy a mammogram machine that you forget.

764
00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:38.500
Oh, wait, maybe A, we can't afford it.

765
00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:40.420
And B, oh, yeah, I have to plug it in.

766
00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:44.020
And I don't know if we can handle all this stuff.

767
00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:48.640
You're going to do anything to divert away from your stupidity, right?

768
00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:50.780
So she's being a cheerleader.

769
00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:52.260
She's trying to get everybody out there.

770
00:29:52.640 --> 00:29:54.120
Come on, let's be happy.

771
00:29:54.260 --> 00:29:56.620
Let's put on our game faces and heal some people.

772
00:29:57.860 --> 00:29:59.020
It's so great.

773
00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.340
But it also, like, it breaks your heart sometimes.

774
00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:08.140
Like, it gets real real, you know, in a way that is something that I always loved about

775
00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:12.640
The Office, too, was that undercurrent of, like, real.

776
00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:14.300
Oh, yeah.

777
00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:20.480
I've cried so many tears watching The Office because there's always something at the end

778
00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:25.560
that's like, and it's just a look or a turn of phrase where you're like, oh, that got me.

779
00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:26.420
That got me.

780
00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:27.380
These are real people.

781
00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:28.840
This is a real situation.

782
00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:30.560
Like, work families.

783
00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:31.760
It's important.

784
00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:35.520
You know, those are—you spend more time with your work family than you do with your real

785
00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:36.680
family, unfortunately.

786
00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:43.020
And, you know, even in the pilot, Alison Tolman's character, which—I met Alison one time.

787
00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:44.400
We both went to the same university.

788
00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:45.800
Not at the same time.

789
00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:46.380
I know.

790
00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:47.460
I'm a dinosaur.

